From: steamlink-ps5
Subject: video: keep decoding through a small frame gap instead of freezing

The remaining "borderline unplayable" stutter on a good link. A whole-path audit
(and two adversarial verifications) traced it to one over-reaction in the video
data channel: DataReceived treated ANY forward frame-sequence gap - even a single
frame - as a reason to request a keyframe, set waitingKeyFrame, and then DROP every
arriving frame until a complete IDR round-tripped. At 60fps that is tens-to-hundreds
of ms of frozen last-frame, and on a good link a 1-3 frame gap (a single lost/late
packet or reorder) fired ~0.7 times/second - a visible mini-freeze about once a
second. The decoder was never the problem (slow submits = 0 all session) and the
packet window stayed healthy (far=0, ovf=0), so patch 0014's wedge fix is intact;
this is purely the drop-until-keyframe recovery policy being far too aggressive.

There is no negotiable keyframe-interval / loss-tolerance field in the protocol
(confirmed: none in remoteplay.proto), so the policy must change in code:

- A small forward gap (<= SOFT_GAP_MAX frames) now asks for a keyframe ONCE to heal
  cleanly, but KEEPS DECODING. A one-frame blip becomes a brief artifact the next
  keyframe wipes, not a multi-frame freeze. The decoder is the backstop: a frame that
  genuinely can't be decoded without its lost reference makes SubmitFrame return
  ReportLost -> ApplyDecoderReset, which still hard-waits, so corruption cannot
  persist. Only a LARGE desync (> SOFT_GAP_MAX) hard-waits for a clean keyframe up
  front, as before.

- The keyframe re-request is coalesced (one outstanding request at a time, tracked by
  keyframeAskedAt) and backed off to KEYFRAME_REREQUEST_MS (500 ms) instead of the old
  flat 200 ms. The 200 ms loop re-sent a full IDR several times a second up the weak
  uplink during a stall, and when a large IDR fragment was itself lost the keyframe
  never assembled and the loop re-fired - the amplifier behind the deepest frame-rate
  dips. keyframeAskedAt is set wherever a keyframe is requested (gap, decrypt failure,
  partial-overflow, decoder reset) and cleared when the keyframe arrives.

Backward (resent/duplicate) sequences are still dropped silently, exactly as patch
0010 established.

